 "findings": [
  "The two selectors read a 0x40-byte pair {+0 tag, +8 word, +0x10 pointer}: sel_park 0x5ce0850 (io/driver.rs:160:32) and sel_timeout 0x5ce0160 (io/driver.rs:165:32). Tag 0 tail-jumps to the forwarder 0x5cfdd00; any other tag takes the fast arm iff [pair+8] == INT64_MIN (0x8000000000000000), jumping to the park body 0x5cfdb90 / park_timeout body 0x5cfd9a0 with rdi = [pair+0x10] + 0x10 and the (secs, nanos) arguments; otherwise it runs slow_a 0x5ceed20, slow_b 0x5ce7380 and tail-jumps slow_c 0x5cee650 on X = pair+8. The forwarder re-tests [arg+0] against INT64_MIN on its own (sentinel immediates at 0x5cfdd8e and 0x5cfdf65) before its own calls to the same three bodies.",
  "Writer census (full .text rel32 + every stored pointer in non-executable sections + every .rela.dyn addend): the pair head is written by exactly one constructor, 0x5ce01f0, behind exactly one installing builder, 0x5cf9600 (builder.rs:2014:54); the only other head stores are the current_thread park entries transient 3 marker. No pinned body except the builder and the two current_thread tops is named by any stored pointer or relocation; the selectors, forwarder, three slow bodies, constructor, poll constructor, waker registration, register bodies and park entry are reached by direct rel32 references only.",
  "The constructor takes its variant and tag bits from two independent bytes of the config the builder builds from its own config u16 at +0xe5: the low byte (constructor config +8) selects the variant, the byte at constructor config +9 selects the tag. The shared write block at 0x5ce0442 writes only tag 0 (byte == 1) or tag 2 (byte != 1) on success.",
  "Parker variant (variant byte 0): Poll::new is never called; the head is {tag, INT64_MIN, pointer}, the pointer being the fresh 0x28-byte parker allocation the RE-005 TLS value-object packet identified (the sentinel is materialised at 0x5ce036d and lands at [pair+8]; the allocation pointer lands at [pair+0x10]).",
  "Poll variant (variant byte != 0): poll_new 0x5ceeae0 builds the poll object {+0 capacity, +8 events buffer, +0x10 0, +0x18 epoll fd, +0x30 the 0x10 key word, +0x38 the 0x80-byte registry buffer, +0x60 fcntl result, +0x64 eventfd} and waker_reg 0x5ce71f0 returns {capacity, events buffer, 0, epoll fd, registration Arc, token}; through the shared write block the installed 0xd8-byte driver value carries the head {tag, capacity, events buffer, 0 count, epoll fd} with the poll object embedded at +0x40.",
  "Tag 3 is an error encoding and is never installed: a poll_new failure (first sret word == INT64_MIN) exits the constructor through 0x5ce06a0 with {[+0] = 3, [+8] = (errno << 32) | 2}; a waker_reg failure (registration word == INT64_MIN, compared at 0x5ce02c2) reaches the same tail. The builder compares the constructed tag against 3 at both call sites (0x5cf967e, 0x5cf9700) and converts a tag-3 result into its own error return; it installs the value (memcpy through slot 0x64df508) only for tags 0 and 2.",
  "current_thread passage: the two top entries (current_thread/mod.rs:449:45 at 0x5cfb610 and :411:45 at 0x5cfb920) snapshot [O+0..0x40) of the stored 0x98-byte object O to the stack, write 3 into [O+0] (panicking \"driver missing\" if it was already 3), call reg_a 0x5cfb780 with the SNAPSHOT as the selector pair, then write the snapshot back. The pair the selectors see therefore carries the pre-existing installed head; the 3 exists only in the stored object while a park is in flight. reg_a also drains the registry waker queue at [S+0x28] after parking.",
  "multi_thread passage: reg_b 0x5cec8b0 (worker.rs:862:41) takes T = [P+8] out of the 0x78-byte worker park object, zeroes [P+8], and forms the pair base as [T+0x10] + 0x10; the park entry 0x5ce2fd0 (multi_thread/park.rs:258:28, 271:18) runs the state-word protocol on [T+0x18] and calls sel_timeout with (0, 0) or the computed (secs, nanos), sel_park when nanos is the sentinel; reg_b also calls sel_park directly at 0x5cec986.",
  "slow_a 0x5ceed20 is the I/O poll (mio event/events.rs:212:9, io/driver.rs:196:23 and :221:17): it converts (secs, nanos) to a saturating millisecond timeout (nanos == sentinel means -1), calls epoll_wait through slot 0x64eb060 with fd [X+0x18], buffer [X+8], maxevents dword [X+0], stores the returned count at [X+0x10], then walks the 12-byte events: data 0 skipped, data 1 sets byte [X+0x1c] = 1, a data pointer CAS-merges event bits into [data+0x10] and calls the wake body 0x5cfd1d0. EINTR returns through helper 0x5cee460; any other failure panics with \".unexpected error when polling the I/O driver: \".",
  "slow_b 0x5ce7380 is the signal drain (signal/mod.rs:118:26, :121:27): it reads byte [X+0x1c], clears it and returns at once unless it was 1; then it drains the self-pipe fd dword [X+0x28] with recv through slot 0x64eb080 (\"EOF on self-pipe\" panic), unregisters the current thread through helpers 0x5ce6df0 / 0x5ce6eb0, and sweeps the global registry at 0x64fee28 (count 0x64fee30, stride 0x20): entries with flag byte +8 set have their object lock word [obj+0x130] CASed 0->8, counter [obj+0x138] incremented by 2, and the callback in slot 0x64ecba8 (-> 0x5cfcc50) invoked on obj+0x10.",
  "slow_c 0x5cee650 is a global cached-object refresh keyed by the pair+0x38 value alone (its argument is pair+0x38, used only as a key): it claims lock byte 0x64fee00 bit 0 (returning at once if already set), returns after unlocking if the cached object 0x64fee08 satisfies [obj+0x138] & ~1 == 0x64fee10, and otherwise looks up a replacement through 0x5cde710 (17-byte key), decrements the old object's Arc count at [obj+0x140], swaps the globals and notifies through 0x5cee900. It references no panic Location.",
  "Answer to the chased question: pairs with discriminator > 1 ARE constructed - tag 2 is one of the two success tags, written by the constructor whenever the builder config tag byte is not 1, in both variants - but the slow path is not selected by the tag. It is selected by [pair+8] != INT64_MIN, i.e. by the poll variant: a tag-2 parker-variant pair takes the fast arm, and the only constructed heads outside the poll variant with [pair+8] != INT64_MIN are the tag-3 error values the builder discards."
 ],
